WEBVTT

00:00:00.035 --> 00:00:03.773
A kickoff is a compact, executable briefing for a new

00:00:03.831 --> 00:00:04.772
agent or session.

00:00:05.375 --> 00:00:09.021
It identifies the project location and baseline, required

00:00:09.091 --> 00:00:13.131
reading, document authority, settled decisions, goal,

00:00:13.212 --> 00:00:17.438
scope, permissions, sequence, checks, and handoff.

00:00:18.042 --> 00:00:21.234
Imagine handing the tracker project to someone who has not

00:00:21.269 --> 00:00:22.825
attended any previous meeting.

00:00:23.208 --> 00:00:25.669
They should be able to locate the spec, know that

00:00:25.727 --> 00:00:28.909
automatic sending is out of scope, and identify the first

00:00:28.943 --> 00:00:29.698
safe task.

00:00:30.139 --> 00:00:32.752
They should not have to reconstruct decisions from a long

00:00:32.822 --> 00:00:33.739
chat transcript.

00:00:34.125 --> 00:00:36.459
You can use multiple agents for independent tasks,

00:00:36.668 --> 00:00:38.537
provided each has a clear assignment.

00:00:38.978 --> 00:00:42.740
Give a worker an outcome, relevant source files, exclusive

00:00:42.763 --> 00:00:45.967
file or subsystem ownership, constraints, acceptance

00:00:46.002 --> 00:00:48.347
checks, and a compact reply format.

00:00:48.951 --> 00:00:51.888
Define shared interfaces before separate workers depend on

00:00:51.934 --> 00:00:52.260
them.

00:00:52.863 --> 00:00:55.870
If one worker implements validation while another works on

00:00:55.917 --> 00:00:58.633
a view, their contract for the inquiry record must be

00:00:58.668 --> 00:00:59.121
agreed.

00:00:59.504 --> 00:01:02.534
A dispatched task is not complete until its result is

00:01:02.581 --> 00:01:03.626
collected and reviewed.

00:01:03.968 --> 00:01:07.625
Route deterministic tasks such as searching, formatting,

00:01:07.799 --> 00:01:10.539
or repeatable checks to ordinary tools when possible.

00:01:11.143 --> 00:01:14.010
Use a qualified local model for suitable extraction or

00:01:14.069 --> 00:01:17.354
drafting, and a capable lower-cost worker for bounded

00:01:17.435 --> 00:01:18.782
ordinary implementation.

00:01:19.479 --> 00:01:21.789
Reserve the strongest available conductor for

00:01:21.859 --> 00:01:26.189
architecture, difficult ambiguity, sensitive boundaries,

00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:28.453
integration, and final judgment.

00:01:29.057 --> 00:01:31.704
Verify what models and tools are actually available.

00:01:32.145 --> 00:01:36.034
A prompt cannot create a model, a capability, or a budget.

00:01:36.638 --> 00:01:39.471
Lower cost is useful only if the route can do the job

00:01:39.529 --> 00:01:40.202
reliably.

00:01:40.591 --> 00:01:43.540
Before substantial dispatch and after a batch of work,

00:01:43.819 --> 00:01:47.186
inspect any available usage information and identify the

00:01:47.244 --> 00:01:48.439
tightest shared allowance.

00:01:48.881 --> 00:01:52.677
Dave's example policy keeps a 25 percent reserve and stops

00:01:52.735 --> 00:01:55.661
discretionary cloud dispatch at 75 percent used.

00:01:56.172 --> 00:01:58.772
These are configurable personal rules, not limits that

00:01:58.807 --> 00:02:00.014
every provider imposes.

00:02:00.456 --> 00:02:02.789
Mac and Windows may share the same account allowance.

00:02:03.230 --> 00:02:06.760
If usage is unavailable, report it as unknown, avoid

00:02:06.806 --> 00:02:10.162
discretionary cloud dispatch, and continue useful work

00:02:10.208 --> 00:02:12.983
with deterministic tools or qualified local capacity.

00:02:13.424 --> 00:02:16.083
Keep only the minimum authorized cloud work needed for

00:02:16.141 --> 00:02:17.743
safe completion and handoff.

00:02:18.184 --> 00:02:20.878
Do not invent a measurement or claim a hard stop is

00:02:20.936 --> 00:02:22.898
enforced when no tool can enforce it.

00:02:23.281 --> 00:02:26.822
Record the route actually used, available usage, results,

00:02:26.869 --> 00:02:27.948
and the next action.

00:02:28.459 --> 00:02:31.966
Avoid filler delegation, paid polling, and repeating a

00:02:32.024 --> 00:02:34.938
check that already passed unless the code changes, a

00:02:35.031 --> 00:02:37.945
failure occurs, or a new concern arises.

00:02:38.328 --> 00:02:41.196
If a worker was interrupted, find out whether it finished

00:02:41.242 --> 00:02:43.135
or changed anything before retrying.

00:02:43.738 --> 00:02:45.816
Never automatically use reset credits.

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:48.765
Account settings, host permissions, and the owner's

00:02:48.812 --> 00:02:50.437
instructions remain authoritative.

00:02:50.791 --> 00:02:53.914
Try: "Implement the tracker validation described in the

00:02:53.949 --> 00:02:54.762
approved spec.

00:02:55.145 --> 00:02:57.815
You own the validation module and its tests.

00:02:58.256 --> 00:03:00.265
Do not change the shared record contract.

00:03:00.590 --> 00:03:01.879
Use synthetic data.

00:03:02.204 --> 00:03:03.562
Run the affected checks.

00:03:03.887 --> 00:03:07.939
Return changed files, results, open risks, and the next

00:03:07.985 --> 00:03:11.364
integration action." That worker has a bounded objective

00:03:11.410 --> 00:03:12.734
and a concrete output.

00:03:13.059 --> 00:03:15.544
The conductor still reads the result and integrates it

00:03:15.578 --> 00:03:18.771
against the whole project rather than treating parallelism

00:03:18.852 --> 00:03:19.909
as automatic proof.

00:03:20.260 --> 00:03:23.348
Start with the exact project and working directory,

00:03:23.673 --> 00:03:27.075
expected base revision, and instructions to preserve

00:03:27.168 --> 00:03:28.398
unrelated changes.

00:03:29.002 --> 00:03:32.206
List the documents in their authority order and summarize

00:03:32.230 --> 00:03:34.134
the decisions that are already settled.

00:03:34.831 --> 00:03:38.674
Describe the approved outcome, exclusions, allowed and

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:42.667
forbidden actions, work sequence, acceptance checks,

00:03:43.109 --> 00:03:44.560
evidence, and handoff.

00:03:45.256 --> 00:03:48.368
If a new session cannot locate a file or reconcile a dirty

00:03:48.414 --> 00:03:52.501
workspace, it should report the specific issue rather than

00:03:52.559 --> 00:03:54.022
assume it can discard work.

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:57.251
Worker one can own the validation module and its tests.

00:03:57.634 --> 00:03:59.852
Worker two can own the owner-view files.

00:04:00.177 --> 00:04:02.917
Their shared contract defines the inquiry identifier,

00:04:03.126 --> 00:04:04.600
fields, and status values.

00:04:04.984 --> 00:04:07.677
The conductor owns the shared contract and integration.

00:04:08.281 --> 00:04:11.415
Each worker gets a compact description, the relevant

00:04:11.474 --> 00:04:15.096
sources, an acceptance check, and the instruction to

00:04:15.189 --> 00:04:17.813
report changed files and unresolved risk.

00:04:18.416 --> 00:04:21.679
If a worker needs to change the shared contract, it should

00:04:21.737 --> 00:04:24.988
coordinate that boundary rather than silently diverge.

00:04:25.283 --> 00:04:27.895
A script can count missing files or run a deterministic

00:04:27.953 --> 00:04:29.288
check without a language model.

00:04:29.729 --> 00:04:32.748
A qualified local model may summarize a bounded source or

00:04:32.818 --> 00:04:34.257
draft a low-risk passage.

00:04:34.699 --> 00:04:37.798
A lower-cost cloud worker may implement a well-specified

00:04:37.845 --> 00:04:38.878
isolated component.

00:04:39.319 --> 00:04:42.106
The strongest available conductor can handle conflicting

00:04:42.187 --> 00:04:45.124
requirements, security boundaries, and integration

00:04:45.171 --> 00:04:45.600
judgment.

00:04:46.041 --> 00:04:49.164
Test model availability before relying on a route, and

00:04:49.234 --> 00:04:51.730
escalate when the work proves harder or riskier than

00:04:51.777 --> 00:04:52.380
expected.

00:04:52.685 --> 00:04:55.703
Suppose a shared allowance is 60 percent used.

00:04:56.087 --> 00:04:59.430
Under Dave's example, there is still room before the 75

00:04:59.523 --> 00:05:02.797
percent discretionary-dispatch cutoff, but the conductor

00:05:02.855 --> 00:05:05.583
should plan the next batch with the 25 percent reserve in

00:05:05.630 --> 00:05:06.002
mind.

00:05:06.606 --> 00:05:08.719
Suppose it is 76 percent used.

00:05:09.102 --> 00:05:12.527
The example policy stops discretionary cloud dispatch and

00:05:12.585 --> 00:05:15.754
preserves capacity for essential completion and handoff.

00:05:16.265 --> 00:05:19.017
It does not assert that every provider has the same window

00:05:19.342 --> 00:05:21.397
or that a percentage represents money.

00:05:21.838 --> 00:05:25.042
If the platform exposes no usage figure, say so.

00:05:25.300 --> 00:05:28.690
After a batch, collect each worker's result, inspect the

00:05:28.748 --> 00:05:32.208
changes and relevant checks, and update the shared state.

00:05:32.649 --> 00:05:35.459
If a worker disappears, determine whether it left a

00:05:35.529 --> 00:05:38.524
commit, modified files, or is still running.

00:05:38.849 --> 00:05:41.450
An interrupted operation may already have produced an

00:05:41.497 --> 00:05:42.402
external effect.

00:05:42.843 --> 00:05:45.212
Investigate before resending or repeating it.

00:05:45.595 --> 00:05:48.346
Avoid paying another worker to reproduce work that was

00:05:48.404 --> 00:05:51.620
already completed, and do not assume a dispatched task

00:05:51.667 --> 00:05:54.952
succeeded merely because no error appeared in the chat.

00:05:55.233 --> 00:05:59.041
Ask workers to return the task outcome, changed files,

00:05:59.169 --> 00:06:03.790
commands and results, evidence location, open risks, and

00:06:03.836 --> 00:06:05.276
the next integration action.

00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:09.455
This makes their output easy to compare and reduces long

00:06:09.513 --> 00:06:10.942
conversational handoffs.

00:06:11.383 --> 00:06:13.983
It does not replace the conductor's responsibility to

00:06:14.053 --> 00:06:17.152
inspect meaningful changes and verify the integration.

00:06:17.663 --> 00:06:20.926
The goal of token efficiency is to spend attention where

00:06:20.949 --> 00:06:23.933
it changes the outcome while keeping enough capacity to

00:06:23.991 --> 00:06:25.070
finish responsibly.

00:06:25.445 --> 00:06:28.475
If the requested premium model is not available, the

00:06:28.533 --> 00:06:31.958
conductor must report the actual model and evaluate

00:06:31.981 --> 00:06:34.385
whether it is capable of the required judgment.

00:06:34.826 --> 00:06:37.856
It can use available tools for independent work and ask

00:06:37.902 --> 00:06:40.631
for help where a genuine capability or authority gap

00:06:40.701 --> 00:06:41.281
remains.

00:06:41.664 --> 00:06:44.613
It should not claim that a model was used simply because a

00:06:44.671 --> 00:06:45.728
prompt requested it.

00:06:46.332 --> 00:06:49.489
The same applies to usage monitoring: an instruction to

00:06:49.547 --> 00:06:52.787
check a quota is not proof that the host exposes a quota.

00:06:53.298 --> 00:06:55.573
Honest reporting is part of the routing contract.
